          <text><![CDATA[<p>You've correctly identified the factor pair {@a@} and {@b@}, but they're the wrong way round.</p>
<p>Remember: the <strong>perfect square</strong> ({@a@}) is the part that goes <em>under</em> the root first, so you can take its square root - it should end up <strong>outside</strong> the root sign as \( \sqrt{ {@a@} } \).</p>
<p>The <strong>square-free</strong> number ({@b@}) is the part that <strong>stays under</strong> the root, since it has no perfect-square factor left to simplify.</p>
<p>Try again: \( \sqrt{ {@number@} } = \sqrt{ {@a@} \times {@b@} } = \sqrt{ {@a@} } \times \sqrt{ {@b@} } \)</p>]]></text>
